#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157084 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65d40bd9-4d35-5e07-a767-8b2ae6ba559a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Union of Sets Without a Venn Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "99f04ab6-00a0-5ccc-a328-49a85547c4c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7153" :question/problem "Given the sets $X = \\{2, 5, 7, 8\\}$, $Y = \\{1, 3, 5\\}$, and $Z = \\{2, 4, 6, 8\\}$, find $X \\cup Y$, $Y \\cup Z$, and $Z \\cup X$." :question/worked-solution "The union $X \\cup Y$ is the set of all elements belonging to either $X$ or $Y$, or both. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX∪Y &= {2, 5, 7, 8}∪{1, 3, 5} \\\\\n&= {1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe union $Y \\cup Z$ is the set of all elements belonging to either $Y$ or $Z$, or both. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nY∪Z &= {1, 3, 5}∪{2, 4, 6, 8} \\\\\n&= {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe union $Z \\cup X$ is the set of all elements belonging to either $Z$ or $X$, or both. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nZ∪X &= {2, 4, 6, 8}∪{2, 5, 7, 8} \\\\\n&= {2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157085 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03cc876e-66d5-51af-ad8d-6c20bc599609" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unions of Sets Given Verbally"}] :question/id #uuid "6a3a434d-5fe4-53cd-ba34-4d10db5c85fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-7154" :question/problem "Let $A$ and $B$ be the set of all multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ and the set of all multiples of $6$ that are greater than $0$, respectively. What is $A \\cup B$?" :question/worked-solution "The multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nA = \\{4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32, 36 \\ldots \\}\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $6$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nB = \\{6,12,18,24,30,36,\\ldots \\}\n$$\n\nNow, $A \\cup B$ is the *union* of $A$ and $B$, which means that we want to find the elements that are in either $A$ or $B$, or both.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA∪B &= {4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28, 32, 36, …}∪{6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36, …} \\\\\n&= {4, 6, 8, 12, 16, 18, 20, 24, 28, 30, 32, 36, …}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157095 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37248a2e-6deb-5b4c-af42-51c358f21110" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Union of Sets Using a Venn Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "3de58ae0-eb3a-5faf-a404-0347d64d4979" :question/math-academy-id "e-7171" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcddc9ee6fe49965ea4f261fcb43c6f7ccd14193910e864b950745ef513e67a6.png)\n\nThe sets $X = \\{1, 2, 3, 5, 8 \\}$ and $Y = \\{1, 2, 4, 8 \\}$ are shown in the Venn diagram above. Find $X\\cup Y$." :question/worked-solution "The union $X \\cup Y$ consists of all the elements that are in $X$ or $Y$, or both. To represent $X \\cup Y$, we will shade in all the area that is contained within either $X$ or $Y$, or both.\n\n![](images/c9/c94cfc38872dfe0673e112e09feec7781ab4f247f08932d1d8ecd167b4a99d32.png)\n\nThe elements $1,2,3,4,5,8$ are in either $X$ or $Y$, or both. Therefore, the union of $X$ and $Y$ is\n\n$$\nX \\cup Y = \\{1,2,3,4,5,8 \\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37248a2e-6deb-5b4c-af42-51c358f21110" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Union of Sets Using a Venn Diagram"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2a78df4-f852-500a-b23e-2d5ff58187c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-102051" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f3d0b514bc4ec701b6ac755b82cec7246a7a819400108e1733759eb0a2fec86.png)\n\nGiven the sets $A={o,p,q,r}$ and $B={q,r,v,w},$ find $A\\cup B.$" :question/worked-solution "The union $A\\cup B$ consists of all the elements that are in either $A$ or $B,$ or both. To represent $A\\cup B,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained in either $A$ or $B,$ or both.\n\n![](images/79/7984b0cb81df816388aa0d5cbf860fc4bdad9f7e871e8671d4616ded02bcaf5b.png)\n\nThe elements $o,p,q,r,v,w$ are in either $A$ or $B$, or both. Therefore, the union of $A$ and $B$ is\n\n$$\nA\\cup B={o,p,q,r,v,w}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03cc876e-66d5-51af-ad8d-6c20bc599609" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unions of Sets Given Verbally"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26c3bb06-29af-54da-a6db-d70352bbb70a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81562" :question/problem "Let $A$ and $B$ be the set of all multiples of $4$ greater than $0$ and the set of all multiples of $5$ greater than $0,$ respectively. What is $A\\cup B?$" :question/worked-solution "The multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nA={4,8,12,16,20,…}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $5$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nB={5,10,15,20,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, $A\\cup B$ is the *union* of $A$ and $B,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in either $A$ or $B,$ or both. In other words, $A\\cup B$ denotes the set of natural numbers that are either multiple of $4$ or multiple of $5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cup B & ={4,8,12,16,20,…}\\cup {5,10,15,20,…} \\\\  & ={4,5,8,10,12,15,16,20,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
